  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nWarm conditions and overall low rain chances will be the rule \nthrough the long term period.\n\nGenerally speaking, the upper-level pattern will be characterized by \nridging over much of the Southeast. A cold air damming (CAD) wedge \nsetup will be in place Tuesday through Wednesday, but the resulting \neasterly flow will do little to moderate temperatures. That said, a \ndecrease in dewpoints is expected Wednesday and Thursday -- in the \n60s compared to the 70s on Tuesday. The overnight/early morning \nhours during this timeframe will also be relatively cooler given the \nslightly drier air.\n\nAt this time, appreciable rain chances will be hard to come by until \nFriday and Saturday, when ensemble guidance suggests that the upper-\nlevel ridge will weaken/retrograde. Even then, the greatest PoPs are \naround 30% and are confined to far north Georgia and parts of \ncentral Georgia both days.",
